  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nWeak sfc boundary shifts low level winds off to the northwest and \n/north tonight into Thursday, as sfc high pressure slides across \nOntario. Another moist night with calming winds and light northwest \nlow level flow will result in more fog, especially across northwest \nlower and eastern upper Michigan (as seen in the SREF and HREF \nvisibility probs). A couple of degrees of cooling is anticipated on \nThursday, due to the high pressure building to the north and the \nnortheast sfc wind shift coming off Lake Huron.\n\nHigh pressure departs to the east later in the week and first half \nof the weekend as heights build aloft. Temperatures will moderate \nback into the 60s most areas, mid-upper 60s for some, with \nprecipitation free weather anticipated.\n\nBy Sunday, deep trough/upper low across Ontario and vicinity will \nbeat down the ridging over northern Michigan to some extent, \nallowing for a trailing frontal boundary to push eastward and \nsouthward across northern MI with time. There will be the potential \nfor some showers along this boundary, especially across the eastern \nUP. Probs are generally low to medium (10-60%) for 0.1\" of rain \nduring this time. Cooler, more seasonably so, air behind this \nfrontal boundary will try to filter in early next week, especially \nTuesday. The degree of the cooldown is a little in question, with \nsome ensemble guidance suggesting near normal temperatures and \nothers a few degrees below. Guidance does show a quick rebound to \nabove normal temperatures, however, after that. During this entire \nperiod, the threat for wetting rains will be minimal. \n\nRumblings of a significant cooldown later this month into early \nNovember making its rounds. Tough to say at this point, but what can \nbe said is significant ridging will build across Alaska (-EPO) which \ngenerally correlates (but not always) to cooler air eventually \nspilling into the lower 48 and Great Lakes region. This to say, most \nensemble guidance means suggest warmer than normal temperatures \nduring this time frame, with a few individual outliers showing a \nmore substantial cool down. Let's watch the trends.",
